WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:02.930
- He is the clever and
very funny political junkie

00:00:02.930 --> 00:00:04.580
you can catch every weeknight

00:00:04.580 --> 00:00:07.185
on "Late Night with Seth Meyers,"

00:00:07.185 --> 00:00:09.330
one of my favorite shows
on TV, not just NBC.

00:00:09.330 --> 00:00:11.670
He is, you guessed it, my friend as well.

00:00:11.670 --> 00:00:13.620
Seth Myers, are you there?

00:00:13.620 --> 00:00:14.453
- Yes!

00:00:14.453 --> 00:00:16.813
And we're matching
denim shirts, I love it.

00:00:16.813 --> 00:00:19.640
- We called each other, I was
like, you wear your denim.

00:00:19.640 --> 00:00:21.830
- Yeah, it's Jay Leno Day.

00:00:21.830 --> 00:00:23.230
- I literally, I know right.

00:00:24.810 --> 00:00:26.960
Actually, this does look
like I'm working on a car,

00:00:26.960 --> 00:00:28.740
it's a whole onesie, but anyway.

00:00:28.740 --> 00:00:29.573
- [Seth] Oh wow.

00:00:29.573 --> 00:00:30.880
- I know, and I only have,

00:00:30.880 --> 00:00:33.055
four things I keep switching through

00:00:33.055 --> 00:00:35.230
because I'm here in
Montana, I have no clothes,

00:00:35.230 --> 00:00:36.130
and I just keep switching

00:00:36.130 --> 00:00:38.450
to just different shirts, it's fine.

00:00:38.450 --> 00:00:40.290
- Yeah, I need to start keeping a list,

00:00:40.290 --> 00:00:43.230
because I'm definitely, I'm
just trying to go two weeks

00:00:43.230 --> 00:00:44.636
before I reuse a shirt on
television, but I'm not sure.

00:00:44.636 --> 00:00:45.469
- Oh.

00:00:45.469 --> 00:00:47.597
- But I think I've had some holes, yeah.

00:00:47.597 --> 00:00:50.010
- I have definitely not accomplished that,

00:00:50.010 --> 00:00:52.610
people are seeing the same
black top over and over again,

00:00:52.610 --> 00:00:54.900
but, I'm in Montana,

00:00:54.900 --> 00:00:57.860
I don't have anything but
warm clothes here, whatever.

00:00:57.860 --> 00:01:00.400
But, so have you had a conversation

00:01:00.400 --> 00:01:02.407
in the past two months
that didn't start with,

00:01:02.407 --> 00:01:04.510
"How's quarantine going?"

00:01:04.510 --> 00:01:06.555
- No, I really haven't.

00:01:06.555 --> 00:01:08.600
(both laughing)

00:01:08.600 --> 00:01:09.800
- I know, I mean it's fascinating,

00:01:09.800 --> 00:01:10.970
there's never been anything

00:01:10.970 --> 00:01:13.800
that everybody's been
experience at once like this.

00:01:13.800 --> 00:01:16.920
You know, you had friends
who cared about some things,

00:01:16.920 --> 00:01:18.180
and friends who cared about other things,

00:01:18.180 --> 00:01:20.560
but it really is, it would be crazy

00:01:20.560 --> 00:01:22.870
to start a conversation without asking.

00:01:22.870 --> 00:01:24.390
- Well, you feel rude, yeah.

00:01:24.390 --> 00:01:26.033
- Yeah.

00:01:26.033 --> 00:01:28.372
And you never know what
the answer is going to be,

00:01:28.372 --> 00:01:29.560
unfortunately, 'cause I
have friends and family

00:01:29.560 --> 00:01:31.160
who have been affected by COVID,

00:01:32.220 --> 00:01:35.457
so even on my most
depressing days, I'm like,

00:01:35.457 --> 00:01:38.390
"Okay, I'm healthy, I'm
healthy," just keep saying that.

00:01:38.390 --> 00:01:40.610
- I feel the same way,
I feel the same way.

00:01:40.610 --> 00:01:43.340
- Yeah, well, we may
be in a different time,

00:01:43.340 --> 00:01:46.170
but I wanted to keep our
day-drinking tradition alive,

00:01:46.170 --> 00:01:48.435
so I brought a beverage.

00:01:48.435 --> 00:01:50.700
Do you have one?

00:01:50.700 --> 00:01:53.079
- I had some grain
alcohol and I finished it.

00:01:53.079 --> 00:01:56.060
- (laughs) Well, that's okay,
- Oh, there's no lid

00:01:56.060 --> 00:01:58.914
I can toast it, I'm
toasting you, here we go.

00:01:58.914 --> 00:02:00.300
- Cheers. (laughs)

00:02:00.300 --> 00:02:03.860
- I'm not gonna lie, this was
left over from Tuesday night,

00:02:03.860 --> 00:02:06.523
the lives in the "The
Voice," it's a little weird.

00:02:08.331 --> 00:02:09.693
But let's, I wanted to mention something,

00:02:09.693 --> 00:02:13.220
because I used a GIF of
you, because I love you,

00:02:13.220 --> 00:02:17.810
and it's funny, so I was
having the worst day,

00:02:17.810 --> 00:02:19.700
my dryer broke, my clothes ended up,

00:02:19.700 --> 00:02:21.240
we have no clothesline,
so I just had to hang them

00:02:21.240 --> 00:02:24.400
on the back porch kind of
area, and they flew off,

00:02:24.400 --> 00:02:27.120
into the forest and I had
to go find all of them.

00:02:27.120 --> 00:02:29.030
And anyway, it was a really rough day,

00:02:29.030 --> 00:02:31.150
and then I got back in
and I had all these texts

00:02:31.150 --> 00:02:32.960
and they were like, "Man, so
now there's murder hornets."

00:02:32.960 --> 00:02:34.430
And I was like, "Wait, what?"

00:02:34.430 --> 00:02:37.500
And I started looking up what
the hell a murder hornet was.

00:02:37.500 --> 00:02:40.563
And then I used your
GIF that was like, sure,

00:02:43.690 --> 00:02:46.500
of course this is happening, why not?

00:02:46.500 --> 00:02:48.900
Have you heard of murder hornets?

00:02:48.900 --> 00:02:52.900
- No, I mean I had on, this weekend

00:02:52.900 --> 00:02:55.470
reading a newspaper article
about it, and again,

00:02:55.470 --> 00:02:56.480
it was just one of those things.

00:02:56.480 --> 00:02:59.070
We did a thing on our show
called "Murder Hornets,"

00:02:59.070 --> 00:03:02.180
which is, we don't have
the bandwidth to be worried

00:03:02.180 --> 00:03:03.693
about you at this moment.

00:03:04.783 --> 00:03:07.450
If you looking to make a
splash, murder hornets,

00:03:07.450 --> 00:03:09.870
you picked the wrong time,
I think you need to do it

00:03:09.870 --> 00:03:12.760
before or after a pandemic,
not in the middle of it.

00:03:12.760 --> 00:03:16.220
I think there are people
who are, at this point,

00:03:16.220 --> 00:03:18.740
would be terrified about
murder hornets any other time,

00:03:18.740 --> 00:03:20.647
that are now like, "Great,
whatever, bring it on,

00:03:20.647 --> 00:03:22.400
"let's just have it all at once."

00:03:22.400 --> 00:03:25.320
- I'm honestly, this happened
to me the other day too,

00:03:25.320 --> 00:03:27.990
there was an earthquake
here, and I didn't know

00:03:27.990 --> 00:03:31.447
earthquakes happened in
Montana, and I was just like,

00:03:31.447 --> 00:03:33.377
"When are the locusts coming?"

00:03:34.960 --> 00:03:38.790
Everything is going to crap,
I don't know what's happening.

00:03:38.790 --> 00:03:41.160
But it's not, that was a bad day.

00:03:41.160 --> 00:03:43.300
But anyway, I just thought it was funny,

00:03:43.300 --> 00:03:45.340
because they're called
murder hornets apparently,

00:03:45.340 --> 00:03:47.520
because they decapitate honeybees.

00:03:47.520 --> 00:03:50.187
They're just taking their
head off, which I was like,

00:03:50.187 --> 00:03:51.320
"of course we would have something

00:03:51.320 --> 00:03:52.950
called a murder hornet right now."

00:03:52.950 --> 00:03:55.100
How is it, doing your show from an attic?

00:03:55.100 --> 00:03:57.180
Why did you choose your
attic, I'm curious?

00:03:57.180 --> 00:03:58.980
- We moved around to a
lot of different rooms.

00:03:58.980 --> 00:04:00.530
we were in a neighbor's garage,

00:04:00.530 --> 00:04:02.640
and that was a little cold and echoey.

00:04:02.640 --> 00:04:05.550
And then I tried a hallway, and that was,

00:04:05.550 --> 00:04:07.380
I looked a little bit like a ghost.

00:04:07.380 --> 00:04:12.380
And then ultimately, this
was both quiet, and also

00:04:13.730 --> 00:04:16.623
the kids couldn't get up here, and then

00:04:16.623 --> 00:04:20.050
just as it started, the
older one got tall enough

00:04:20.050 --> 00:04:22.870
to lift the latch, and so
now every now and then,

00:04:22.870 --> 00:04:25.290
the stairs are sort of right
there, every now and then

00:04:25.290 --> 00:04:30.290
I'll see his little towhead
pop up, and just one of these.

00:04:31.320 --> 00:04:34.270
And it's really, the most
embarrassing thing about this,

00:04:34.270 --> 00:04:37.276
is that my kids know that I do a show,

00:04:37.276 --> 00:04:39.793
that's Daddy's job, is he does a show.

00:04:40.710 --> 00:04:43.130
And now I'm worried that
they think all the times

00:04:43.130 --> 00:04:45.530
I've left our apartment in
New York to go to 30 Rock

00:04:45.530 --> 00:04:48.370
they think I'm also just going
into a weird little attic.

00:04:48.370 --> 00:04:52.040
I wanna be like, no there's
a big, Daddy's a big deal.

00:04:52.040 --> 00:04:54.225
- Yeah, I'm a big deal, yeah.

00:04:54.225 --> 00:04:56.100
- There's a big, there's lights.

00:04:56.100 --> 00:04:57.360
- I know, but it's weird, your kids

00:04:57.360 --> 00:04:59.510
are going to start doing
this because River and Remy,

00:04:59.510 --> 00:05:02.310
the younger ones of our
four, they start to think

00:05:02.310 --> 00:05:06.690
that everyone's Mom and Dad
do the same kind of thing.

00:05:06.690 --> 00:05:10.130
So they don't understand it quite yet,

00:05:10.130 --> 00:05:12.507
they are four and five, so
they kind of think like,

00:05:12.507 --> 00:05:14.047
"Well everybody has a
studio in their house

00:05:14.047 --> 00:05:16.707
"and everyone has a mom who
sits in front of a camera

00:05:16.707 --> 00:05:17.710
"and talks to a bunch of people."

00:05:17.710 --> 00:05:19.920
It's such a funny thing to watch kids,

00:05:19.920 --> 00:05:22.180
kind of get it.
- The funny thing, though

00:05:22.180 --> 00:05:23.827
is when it's over and then they say,

00:05:23.827 --> 00:05:26.887
"Why are you going to
work, you did it at home.

00:05:26.887 --> 00:05:29.047
"I just think you want to leave us

00:05:29.047 --> 00:05:30.780
"for eight hours"
- Think you want to leave us.

00:05:30.780 --> 00:05:32.732
- I'll have to have to
sit them down and say,

00:05:32.732 --> 00:05:34.920
"that is why, I want to be away,

00:05:34.920 --> 00:05:36.987
in order to love you as much we need to,

00:05:36.987 --> 00:05:38.610
"we have to be away from you."

00:05:38.610 --> 00:05:41.423
- You do need alone time,
I am with you on that.

00:05:42.737 --> 00:05:45.100
All jokes aside, do you
like working at home?

00:05:45.100 --> 00:05:48.030
Or has it been, its
been exhausting for me.

00:05:48.030 --> 00:05:50.670
- It's exhausting, I mean,
I miss the camaraderie.

00:05:50.670 --> 00:05:53.210
I've always valued how
luck I am to have a job

00:05:53.210 --> 00:05:54.230
where you are surrounded by people.

00:05:54.230 --> 00:05:56.290
Creative people, people
who make you laugh.

00:05:56.290 --> 00:05:59.660
So I miss that a lot, I
also feel lucky enough

00:05:59.660 --> 00:06:02.010
that there is a way to do
it, that we figured out

00:06:02.010 --> 00:06:03.260
a way to do it from home.

00:06:04.264 --> 00:06:06.370
But that part of it is the hardest part,

00:06:06.370 --> 00:06:07.290
not being around people.

00:06:07.290 --> 00:06:08.190
- [Kelly] I agree.

00:06:09.276 --> 00:06:10.460
But watching your show, I've
noticed the stack of books

00:06:10.460 --> 00:06:12.572
next to you, so is that your reading list,

00:06:12.572 --> 00:06:13.955
or are you just trying to look impressive?

00:06:13.955 --> 00:06:15.600
- I'm just trying to look impressive.

00:06:15.600 --> 00:06:18.300
I highly recommend "The Thorn
Birds," that's a good book.

00:06:18.300 --> 00:06:21.310
- I've actually read that, and I actually,

00:06:21.310 --> 00:06:24.601
they made a series on it, I've seen it

00:06:24.601 --> 00:06:26.143
- Miniseries, yeah, 1983.

00:06:26.143 --> 00:06:30.204
This is a very little red
"Thorn Birds 2, More Thorns"

00:06:30.204 --> 00:06:31.857
- (laughs) I love...

00:06:31.857 --> 00:06:33.517
- And an almost never read,

00:06:33.517 --> 00:06:37.000
"Thorn Birds 3, Things
be Getting Thornier."

00:06:37.000 --> 00:06:38.020
That was a very--

00:06:38.020 --> 00:06:40.003
- I actually did (noise
drowns out voice) read it.

00:06:40.003 --> 00:06:41.092
That's so funny.

00:06:41.092 --> 00:06:42.429
- Very badly reviewed book.

00:06:42.429 --> 00:06:44.394
- I love you for it.

00:06:44.394 --> 00:06:47.785
- I basically think that,
people have pointed out,

00:06:47.785 --> 00:06:50.742
we're basically seeing
everybody's bookshelf,

00:06:50.742 --> 00:06:53.140
and that's just way too much,

00:06:53.140 --> 00:06:54.890
I don't want to reveal
that much about myself.

00:06:54.890 --> 00:06:57.640
So it's mostly every
book I put up has been

00:06:59.920 --> 00:07:02.120
either books I love or a joke.

00:07:02.120 --> 00:07:05.099
Like, I put up "Common
Sense Pest Control,"

00:07:05.099 --> 00:07:08.021
I thought that was...

00:07:08.021 --> 00:07:09.410
(both chattering)

00:07:09.410 --> 00:07:11.170
- I'm gonna start doing
it, and I'm going to put

00:07:11.170 --> 00:07:12.410
my favorite childhood ones,

00:07:12.410 --> 00:07:14.600
my world doll collection or something.

00:07:14.600 --> 00:07:16.460
- I think, and again, I don't know,

00:07:16.460 --> 00:07:17.770
I don't want to judge too hard because

00:07:17.770 --> 00:07:20.270
I've never written a book,
and if I wrote a book

00:07:20.270 --> 00:07:21.840
I might very well put it behind me.

00:07:21.840 --> 00:07:24.151
But I think it's funny
the amount of people,

00:07:24.151 --> 00:07:26.251
their book is behind them.

00:07:26.251 --> 00:07:28.060
- Their own thing, isn't
that the worst though?

00:07:28.060 --> 00:07:30.530
When you're, I'm so
bad at that in general,

00:07:30.530 --> 00:07:33.390
in this industry, even for now 20 years.

00:07:33.390 --> 00:07:35.860
I'm so bad at promoting myself, I hate it.

00:07:35.860 --> 00:07:38.679
I feel like such a tool,
even if it's my song,

00:07:38.679 --> 00:07:40.737
I'm like, "Yeah, either
listen to it or don't,

00:07:40.737 --> 00:07:43.301
"or read my children's book or don't."

00:07:43.301 --> 00:07:46.330
I'm the worst salesmen ever, but I do,

00:07:46.330 --> 00:07:48.820
I laugh at that too, or when
you go to someone's house,

00:07:48.820 --> 00:07:51.410
and there's a wall of all their accolades,

00:07:51.410 --> 00:07:55.110
right when you walk in, and
there's nothing wrong with it,

00:07:55.110 --> 00:07:59.590
but I'm like, "Mine's
in my attic." (laughs)

00:07:59.590 --> 00:08:02.300
- I think, right, I think
there's a happy medium

00:08:02.300 --> 00:08:04.520
between foyer and attic, and you know,

00:08:04.520 --> 00:08:07.020
I encourage you to bring
it a little farther down.

00:08:07.890 --> 00:08:10.863
You know, for me, I like
to, and by the way I think

00:08:10.863 --> 00:08:13.680
I have fewer accolades, but
you know, put it somewhere

00:08:13.680 --> 00:08:15.820
little Easter egg for people.

00:08:15.820 --> 00:08:18.170
- Yeah, no, it's just funny,
right when you walk in

00:08:18.170 --> 00:08:20.290
I'm like, "Well, you're proud, it's cool."

00:08:20.290 --> 00:08:22.200
- So, are your kids,
are your boys enjoying

00:08:22.200 --> 00:08:23.677
that you are home everyday, do you get to,

00:08:23.677 --> 00:08:25.530
because I get to do a lot more things

00:08:25.530 --> 00:08:28.020
than I normally would with them.

00:08:28.020 --> 00:08:30.840
- Yeah, it's been great, it's
genuinely has been great.

00:08:30.840 --> 00:08:34.090
And out of necessity, we
have to upload the show

00:08:34.090 --> 00:08:36.440
a lot earlier than we would
have to in the studio,

00:08:36.440 --> 00:08:39.040
so I have way more time with them.

00:08:39.040 --> 00:08:41.344
And the whole first month,
I just kept thinking,

00:08:41.344 --> 00:08:44.250
I'm so glad I get to put
my kids to bed at night,

00:08:44.250 --> 00:08:47.053
and now, ideally, I'll never do it again.

00:08:48.160 --> 00:08:50.093
- I feel like I knocked it out.

00:08:50.990 --> 00:08:52.280
Also, my son, the four-year-old,

00:08:52.280 --> 00:08:55.860
does an hour of Zoom a day for school,

00:08:55.860 --> 00:08:57.570
because he's just four.

00:08:57.570 --> 00:08:59.580
- No my kid does the same thing, yeah.

00:08:59.580 --> 00:09:02.690
- Today he yelled because
he pressed something,

00:09:02.690 --> 00:09:04.830
he was Zooming on an
iPad, and he screamed that

00:09:04.830 --> 00:09:08.370
he had turned it off, and
then I went down to fix it,

00:09:08.370 --> 00:09:10.479
and he was just sitting,
he had his pants off,

00:09:10.479 --> 00:09:12.772
and I was like, "Did
you take your pants off

00:09:12.772 --> 00:09:14.257
after you turned it off?"
And he goes, "yeah."

00:09:14.257 --> 00:09:16.650
And I was like, 'Thank
god, because you might

00:09:16.650 --> 00:09:21.370
be on a watch list, so
let's get your pants back on

00:09:21.370 --> 00:09:22.688
and turn--

00:09:22.688 --> 00:09:23.570
- That's the most amazing thing

00:09:23.570 --> 00:09:25.980
you'll see from reporters,
from their home.

00:09:25.980 --> 00:09:27.180
The one guy was in his shorts

00:09:27.180 --> 00:09:30.180
and he had no idea the screen
was that wide, that's amazing.

